REGIONAL REGULATION OF LAMANDAU REGENCY

NUMBER 16 OF 2015

CONCERNING

INTEGRATED SERVICES FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN EMPOWERMENT

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF LAMANDAU,

Considering:

a. that acts of violence against women and children that can cause physical and psychological victims constitute violations of human rights, discrimination against women and children and crimes against human dignity thus hindering the creation of justice and gender equality;
b. that protection for women and children victims of violence is an effort to provide a sense of security and freedom from all acts of violence guaranteed by laws and regulations;
c. that the regional government is obliged to regulate and manage the handling and provide services for women and children victims of violence which include law enforcement, legal protection, health services, and spiritual aspects;
d. that to improve protection for women and children victims of violence, it is necessary to establish a facility that can provide integrated services;
e. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, letter c and letter d, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation of Lamandau Regency concerning Integrated Services for Women and Children Empowerment.

In View of:

1. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention On The Elimination Of All Form Of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3668;
3. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO CONVENTION Number 138 Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 5 of 2002 concerning Establishment of Katingan Regency, Seruyan Regency, Sukamara Regency, Lamandau Regency, Gunung Mas Regency, Pulang Pisau Regency, Murung Raya Regency, East Barito Regency in Central Kalimantan Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 18, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4180);
6. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 Concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
9. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007, Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
10. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning Formation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234;
11. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
12. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006, Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
13. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses And/Or Victims of Criminal Acts of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
14. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 1 of 2014 concerning Formation of Legislation (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 32);
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2009 concerning Guidelines for Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers.

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING INTEGRATED SERVICES FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN EMPOWERMENT.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Integrated services for women and children empowerment are carried out based on Pancasila, the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, as well as the principles and basic rights as regulated in the Convention on women and children, which include:
a. Respect for human rights;
b. Gender justice and equality;
c. Non-discrimination;
d. The best interests of women and or children;
e. The right to life, survival, and development;
f. Respect for the opinions of women and children;
g. Gender justice and equality; and
h. Protection of victims.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS AND OBLIGATIONS

Article 4

The rights of children are as follows:
a. Every child has the right to live, grow, develop and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and worth, and to receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. Every child has the right to a name as identity and citizenship status;
c. Every child has the right to worship according to their religion, to think and express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age, under the guidance of parents;
d. Every child has the right to know their parents, to be raised and cared for by their own parents;
e. In the event that for any reason the parents cannot guarantee the child's growth and development, or the child is in a state of neglect, then the child has the right to be cared for and adopted as a foster child or adopted child by another person in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations;
f. Every child has the right to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual and social needs;
g. Every child has the right to obtain education and teaching in the context of developing their personality and level of intelligence in accordance with their interests and talents;
h. Especially for children with disabilities, they have the right to obtain special education, while children who have excellence have the right to receive special education;
i. Every child has the right to express and be heard their opinion, to receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with their level of intelligence and age for their self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;
j. Every child has the right to rest and utilize leisure time, to associate with peers, to play, to recreate, and to create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development;
k. Every child with disabilities has the right to obtain rehabilitation, social assistance, and maintenance of social welfare standards; and
l. Every child while in the care of parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for care, has the right to receive protection from treatment of Discrimination; Exploitation, both economic and sexual; Neglect;  Cruelty, violence, and abuse;  Injustice; and Other mistreatment.
m. Every child has the right to be cared for by their own parents, unless there are valid reasons and/or legal rules showing that the separation is in the best interests of the child and is a last consideration.
n. Every child has the right to obtain protection from: Abuse in political activities;  Involvement in armed conflict; Involvement in social unrest; Involvement in events containing elements of violence; and Involvement in warfare.
o. Every child has the right to obtain protection from being the target of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment.
p. Every child has the right to obtain freedom in accordance with the law.
q. Arrest, detention, or criminal imprisonment of a child shall only be carried out if in accordance with applicable law and can only be carried out as a last resort.
r. Every child deprived of liberty has the right to: Receive humane treatment and placement separated from adults;  Obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal remedies; and - Defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial juvenile court in a closed hearing for law.
s. Every child who is a victim or perpetrator of sexual violence or who is in conflict with the law has the right to confidentiality.
t. Every child who is a victim or perpetrator of a criminal act has the right to receive legal assistance and other assistance.

Article 5

Victims of domestic violence have the right to receive:
a. Protection from family, police, prosecution, courts, legal aid, social institutions, or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the court;
b. Health services in accordance with medical needs;
c. Special handling related to victim confidentiality;
d. Assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and
e. Spiritual guidance services.

Article 6

Every child has the obligation to:
a. Respect parents, guardians, and teachers;
b. Love family, community, and care for friends;
c. Love the homeland, nation and state;
d. Perform worship in accordance with the teachings of their religion; and
e. Practice noble ethics and morals.

Article 7

Every person is prohibited from committing domestic violence against persons within their household scope, by means of:
a. Physical violence;
b. Psychological violence;
c. Sexual violence; and
d. Household neglect.

Article 13

1. Regional governments are obliged to organize basic education of at least 9 (nine) years for all children.
2. The State, government, families and parents are obliged to provide the widest possible opportunity for children to obtain education. (3) Education is directed at:
a. Development of attitudes and abilities of the child's personality, talents, mental and physical abilities to achieve their optimal potential;
b. Development of respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms;
c. Development of respect for parents, cultural identity, language and their own values, national values where the child resides, from where the child originates, and civilizations that differ from one's own civilization;
d. Preparation of children for a responsible life; and
e. Development of respect and love for the environment.
4. Children with physical and/or mental disabilities are given equal opportunities and accessibility to obtain regular education and special education.
5. Children in and around schools must be protected from acts of violence committed by teachers, school administrators or their friends in the school concerned or other educational institutions.

Article 14

1. The Government is obliged to organize maintenance and care of neglected children, both in institutions and outside social institutions.
2. The Government in organizing maintenance and care is obliged to strive for and assist children, so that children can:
a. Participate;
b. Freely express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;
c. Freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the age and development stage of the child;
d. Freely associate and assemble;
e. Freely rest, play, recreate, create and engage in cultural arts; and
f. Obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.

Article 22

In providing services, social workers must:
a. Conduct counseling to strengthen and provide a sense of security for the victim;
b. Provide information regarding the victim's rights to obtain protection from the police and a protection order decree from the court;
c. Escort the victim to a safe house or alternative residence; and
d. Conduct integrated coordination in providing services to victims with the police, social services, social institutions needed by the victim.

Article 23

In providing services, volunteer companions may:
a. Inform the victim of their right to obtain one or more companions;
b. Accompany the victim at the investigation, prosecution or court examination level by guiding the victim to objectively and completely describe the domestic violence they experienced;
c. Listen empathetically to all the victim's statements so that the victim feels safe being accompanied by the companion; and
d. Actively provide psychological and physical strengthening to the victim.

Article 28

An application to obtain a protection order may be submitted by:
a. The victim or the victim's family;
b. A friend of the victim;
c. The Police;
d. Support volunteers; and
e. A spiritual counselor.

Article 29 (1) An application for a protection order shall be submitted in oral or written form.

2. In the event the application is submitted orally, the clerk of the local district court shall record the application.
3. In the event an application for a protection order is submitted by family, a friend of the victim, the police, support volunteers, or a spiritual counselor, the victim must give consent. In certain circumstances, an application may be submitted without the victim's consent.
